The single-orientation constraint on the positive strand restricts every meiosis to producing only the four forward-switch haplotypes (00001, 00011, 00111, 01111). Because an F2 individual is formed by receiving one such haplotype and the second meiosis is subject to the identical polarity restriction, the F3 output remains inside exactly the same set of four strings. No mechanism exists under the stated rules to recover the opposite polarity, additional transitions, or the pure parentals.

Thus exactly four distinct sequences are possible.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {4}

