4.text:
The single-polarity constraint on the positive strand limits each meiosis to choosing exactly one of the four inter-SNP intervals at which to place its obligatory crossover. After the first meiosis the admissible strings are therefore only the four unidirectional single-switch haplotypes (00001, 00011, 00111, 01111).  

An F2 individual carries any ordered pair drawn from that set. In the second meiosis the identical interval set remains available. When the chosen interval is the same as the interval that already exists on one of the F2 chromosomes, the two crossovers cancel, restoring a pure parental haplotype. When the chosen interval is distinct, the resulting gamete contains exactly two transitions. Consequently every binary string of length 5 that contains at most two transitions is reachable, while strings requiring three or more transitions remain impossible. Enumeration yields the familiar total of 22 attainable sequences.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {22}

