4.text:
The unidirectional polarity constraint on the positive strand restricts every meiosis to a single obligatory forward (0-to-1) crossover in one of the four intervals. Consequently the only haplotypes that can ever be generated—whether in the first or second meiosis—are exactly the four single-transition strings 00001, 00011, 00111 and 01111.  

An F2 chromosome must already belong to this set. When that chromosome undergoes the second meiosis, the same polarity rule again permits only one forward crossover; the resulting gamete therefore remains inside the identical four-element set. No reversal, same-interval cancellation, or additional transition is mechanistically possible, so the F3 pool contains precisely these four sequences.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {4}

